3497665573
section  4248078621
Several notable properties of condensed matter phases are defined by fundamental physical constants.
The Bohr radius gives a characteristic scale of interatomic distance on the order of the Angstrom, in terms of electron mass   76726987  , charge   816449627  , and Planck constant   340463602  .
These same fundamental constants enter the Rydberg energy, setting the scale of a characteristic bonding energy in condensed phases and chemical compounds   429917333  .
Among the fundamental constants, those that are   1573268529   and do not depend on the choice of units, play a special role in physics   2581068005  .
Two important dimensionless constants are the fine structure constant   2122471043   and the proton-to-electron mass ratio,   3184337359  .
The finely-tuned values of   2122471043   and   3184337359  , and the balance between them, governs nuclear reactions such as proton decay and nuclear synthesis in stars, leading to the creation of the essential biochemical elements, including carbon.
This balance provides a narrow ``habitable zone'' in the   3814575437   space where stars and planets can form and life-supporting molecular structures can emerge   2581068005  .
We show that a simple combination of   2122471043   and   3184337359   results in another dimensionless quantity which has an unexpected and specific implication for a key property of condensed phases, the speed at which waves travel in solids and liquids, or the speed of sound,   1329155215  .
We find that this combination provides an upper bound for   1329155215  ,   3730642209  , as
578174126
begin  3119931285     3516303764  =\alpha  1719989662  ^  3179439190  , \label  393140867   \end  3119931285   \noindent where   514015864   is the speed of light in vacuum.
We support this result with a large set of experimental data for different systems, and the first principles modelling of atomic hydrogen.
Identifying and understanding bounds on physical properties is important from the point of view of fundamental physics, predictions for theory and experiment, as well as searching for and rationalizing universal behavior   758032211  .
Properties for which bounds were recently discussed include viscosity and diffusivity.
The proposed   632748636   bounds for these two properties feature in a range of areas including, for example, strongly-interacting field theories, quark-gluon plasmas, holographic duality, electron diffusion, transport properties in metals and superconductors, and spin transport in Fermi gases   2675183149  .
Recently, two of us found a lower bound for the kinematic viscosity of liquids set by fundamental physical constants   3918824806  .
Here, we propose a new,   1163739713  , bound for the speed of sound in condensed matter phases in terms of fundamental constants.
Apart from setting the speed of elastic interactions in solids,   1329155215   is related to elasticity, hardness and affects important low-temperature thermodynamic properties such as energy, entropy and heat capacity   3103754819  .
As discussed below, the upper bound of   1329155215   sets the smallest possible entropy and heat capacity at a given temperature.
In solids,   1329155215   depends on elastic properties and density.
These strongly depend on the bonding type and structure which are inter-dependent   4016372219  .
As a result, it was not thought that   1329155215   can be predicted analytically without simulations, contrary to other properties such as energy or heat capacity which are universal in the classical harmonic approximation   3103754819  .
In view of this, representing the upper bound of   1329155215   in terms of fundamental constants is notable.
