Before discussing the experimental data in relation to Eq.   3137161627   and its consequences, Eqs.   2982880790  -  586812962  , we note that the speed of sound is governed by the elastic moduli and density which substantially vary with bonding type: from strong covalent, ionic, or metallic bonding, typically giving a large bonding energy to intermediate hydrogen-bonding, and weak dipole and van der Waals interactions.
Elastic moduli and density also vary with the particular structure that a system adopts.
Furthermore, the bonding type and structure are themselves inter-dependent: covalent and ionic bonding result in open and close-packed structures, respectively   4016372219  .
As a result, the speed of sound for a particular system can not be predicted analytically and without the explicit knowledge of structure and interactions   4198969183  , similarly to other system-dependent properties such as viscosity or thermal conductivity   847664205  .
Nevertheless, the dependence of   1329155215   on   158786288   or   1338835853   can be studied in a family of elemental solids.
Elemental solids do not have confounding features existing in compounds due to mixed bonding between different atomic species   1756941070  .
To compare Eq.   586812962   to experiments, we plot the available data of   1329155215   as a function of   1338835853   for 36 elemental solids   1156064554   in Fig.   2262842779  , including semiconductors and metals with large bonding energies.
The data are depicted in a log-log plot.
Equation   586812962   is the straight line in Fig.   2262842779   ending in its upper theoretical bound   2982880790   for   2960855925  .
The linear Pearson correlation coefficient calculated for the experimental set   4080144166   is   2305504446  .
Its absolute value is slightly above the boundary notionally separating moderate and strong correlations   4168279224  .
The ratio of calculated and experimental   1329155215   is in the range 0.6-2.4, consistent with the range of   4065160191   approximated by 1 in the derivation of Eq.   3177061649  .
