Similarly to solids,   1329155215   in liquids satisfy the bound   3730642209  .
We note that our evaluation of   1329155215   and   3730642209   applies to liquids with cohesive states   2779809631  , where molecular dynamics includes solid-like oscillatory components   3467156894   and where   1329155215   is set by the elastic moduli as in solids, albeit taken at their high-frequency   3485658016   values   2174282748  .
On the other hand, at high temperature and/or low density, cohesive states are lost and Eq.   2112336645   and Eq.   183019717   and our derivation of   1329155215   do not apply.
In this regime, the moduli are related to the kinetic energy of molecules rather than interactions and bonding energy, and   1329155215   starts to increase with temperature and loses its universality.
Above the Frenkel line   1902411760  , formalising the qualitative change of molecular dynamics from combined oscillatory and diffusive to purely diffusive,   1329155215   is equal to the thermal speed of molecules as in a gas.
With regard to liquids, we note that an expression similar to   3177061649   was earlier obtained by evaluating the elastic modulus using the liquid state theory and applied to liquid metals   4196151825  .
The speed of sound can also be evaluated in the theory of metals using the ionic plasma frequency and subsequently accounting for the conduction electrons screening.
This results in the Bohm-Staver relation   3928063118  , where   3771832551   is the Fermi velocity   429917333  , and hence   430131255   as in Eq.   586812962     3521778858  .
These and other relations derived for the liquid state give a fairly good account of the experimental sound velocity in liquid metals   4270318261  .
We make three further remarks about the calculated   1329155215   and its bound.
First, this derivation involves approximations as mentioned earlier.
The approximations may affect the numerical factor in Eqs.   3137161627   and   2982880790  .
At the same time, the characteristic scale of   1329155215   in   3137161627   and its upper bound   2982880790   is set by fundamental constants.
Second, Eq.   2112336645   as well as Eqs.   183019717  -  3922673172   used in the second derivation of   1329155215   assume valence electrons directly involved in bonding and hence strongly-bonded systems, including metallic, covalent and ionic solids.
Although bonding in weakly-bonded solids such as noble, molecular and hydrogen-bonded solids is also electromagnetic in origin, weak dipole and van der Waals interactions result in smaller   2227979358     3329069466   and smaller   1329155215   as a result.
Therefore, the upper bound   3730642209   applies to weakly-bonded systems too.
We note here that our evaluation does not directly distinguish between bonding types and hence does not consider the trend of   1329155215   to increase along the rows of the periodic table, from soft metals to hard covalent materials in Fig.   2262842779  .
This trend can be accounted for by   3600421781   noting that   1329155215   in   963562228   and   1945425016   imply   1925260931   and   1441744645   introducing an extra parameter into the equation for   1329155215   related to density   1669955715  .
Third, our evaluation of   1329155215   does not account for the effect of pressure on   2227979358   and   1156178167   and applies when the enthalpic term is relatively small.
%Fourth, although weakly-bonded systems with heavy molecules correspond to lower   1329155215  , estimating a lower bound of   1329155215   is complicated by chemical variety of weak bonding as well as extrinsic effects such as defects.
Our upper bound in Eq.   2982880790   corresponds to solid hydrogen with strong metallic bonding.
Although this phase only exists at megabar pressures   4097396754   and is dynamically unstable at ambient pressure where molecular formation occurs, it is interesting to calculate   1329155215   in atomic hydrogen in order to check the validity of our upper bound.
In addition, there has been strong interest in the properties of atomic hydrogen at high pressure   3792236121  , although the speed of sound in these phases was not discussed and remains unknown.
We have calculated the speed of sound in atomic hydrogen for the   3469282951   structure   3276497929  , which is currently the best candidate structure for solid atomic metallic hydrogen.
This structure is calculated to become thermodynamically stable in the pressure range   768167687  --  780021899   GPa   2269651173  , below which solid hydrogen is a molecular solid.
However, we find that   3469282951   is dynamically stable at pressures above about   3566698918   GPa, and therefore we perform calculations in the pressure range   3566698918  --  3899602956   GPa.
The speed of sound as a function of pressure and density reported in Fig.   1349764476   corresponds to the highest energy acoustic branch and is averaged over stochastically generated directions in   2801857405  -space.
