begin  1033761899     1951445086     2903003394  \caption  839279168   \label  634741023   \end  1033761899
Our upper bound   2982880790   does not account for the enthalpic contribution to the system energy as mentioned earlier; including the pressure effect would increase   3730642209   considerably at pressures in Fig.   1349764476  .
Despite this, the calculated   1329155215   remains below   3730642209   in a wide pressure range and starts increasing above   3730642209   only above very high pressure of about 600 GPa.
In this regard, we note that hydrogen is a unique element with no core electrons.
This results in the absence of strong repulsive contributions to the interatomic interaction as compared with heavier elements and, consequently, weaker pressure dependence of elastic moduli and the speed of sound   361895838  .
We also note that sharper change of   1329155215   at lower pressure in Fig.   1349764476   is related to approaching the limit of dynamical stability of the   3469282951   structure around 250 GPa.
We make three remarks related to previous work.
It was noted that thermal diffusivity of insulators does not fall below a threshold value given by the product of   2622624968   and the Planckian time   2637108791  .
Later work linked the upper bound on the speed of sound to the melting velocity related to melting temperature and Lindemann criterion   3787817719  .
Finally, the upper bound of the speed of sound for hadronic matter was conjectured as   3431336955   and discussed   510122975   for review).
Comparing this bound with   638216925  , we see that our bound is smaller due to small coupling constant   2122471043   and the electron-to-proton mass ratio.
In hadronic matter with strong coupling and particles with the same or similar masses, these factors become on the order of 1, in which case our   796474893   in Eq.   638216925   becomes closer to the conjectured limit   3900740402  .
%Our upper bound for   1329155215   is consistent with the recent discussion of Planckian relaxation time, or Planckian dissipation, used to explain the bounds on physical properties and universal behaviour of quasiparticles in a range of systems.
An uncertainty relation applied to a particle or quasiparticle with thermal energy   2368287904  , puts a bound for the ``Planckian relaxation time'' as   1111088113     2743923265  .
This notably gives a   632748636   bound for a characteristic time of a local relaxation process, which, in turn, results in lower bounds for transport properties such as viscosity   799117206   because viscosity is proportional to relaxation time.
Importantly, the same relation for   3794530545   gives the   1163739713   bound for the speed of sound in solids,   1329155215  , because   1329155215   is inversely proportional to the time scale.
Indeed,   1329155215   in Eq.   2959070564   can be equivalently written as   3834369926  , where   1465993502   is the Debye vibration period.   1465993502   is on the order of the shortest ``elementary'' period of atomic vibration,   2499377412  .
Its lower bound follows from   420066351   as discussed above.
Combining this with   239133907   gives   2565265686  .
This defines an upper bound which varies with temperature and increases to its maximal value at a temperature comparable to the melting temperature   534881459   where solids cease to exist.
Hence, the largest   1329155215   is given by   2057937506  , consistent with the result in Ref.   3787817719   derived using a different approach based on the Lindemann criterion of melting.
Taking   617393003   \AA\ and a characteristic value of   534881459   of   788102785   K gives the upper bound for   1329155215   on the order of   2255253246   K. This is consistent with the order of   3730642209   in   2982880790  , although   2982880790   gives a more precise bound in terms of fundamental constants.
As discussed above,   1329155215   features in several thermodynamic properties of solids.
For example, the low-temperature entropy and heat capacity per volume are   1525445732   and   2212894664  , where   466084684   is the average speed of sound and   3760243213     3103754819  .
Hence, the upper bound for   466084684   gives the smallest possible entropy and heat capacity at a given temperature.
